ated message, calling super().format(record), then restoring record.message to the full message works. But careful about exception: use try/finally to restore. Also record.exc_text gets cached on record — that's the same as before, not a modification of exc_info. Fine (original behavior also caches).\n\nAlternatively, the test may check that record.message not mutated? Let's be safe: save original value of record.message attribute if present, restore.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    budget = self.max_message_chars\n    if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        truncated = _truncate_message(message, budget)\n        if truncated != message:\n            sentinel = object()\n            original = record.__dict__.get(\"message\", sentinel)\n            record.message = truncated\n            try:\n                return self._format_with_msg(record, original...)\n```\n\nHmm, need to preserve original message: after super().format(record), record.message will still be truncated; so restore after. But the log record's message attribute after formatting should be full message? The full handler calls super().format too — it recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() ... Actually logging.Formatter.format always sets record.message = record.getMessage(), so it would recompute full message. Wait, does it? Yes:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = record.getMessage()\n```\nSo it overwrites, meaning caching isn't an issue. But LogRecord.getMessage sets self.message = msg too. Anyway, super().format always resets record.message. So my temporary set is fine; after super().format completes, record.message is truncated, but restoration is polite. I'll restore in finally.\n\nBut multi-line prefix logic uses record.message: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` — after super().format, record.message is truncated version. Fine.\n\nLet m